PanNuke Dataset Analysis 

We have completed the dataset analysis for the PanNuke dataset that we'll use for the project.

1. Dataset structure

We are using the original three PanNuke folds:

PanNuke
├── Fold 1 → Training
├── Fold 2 → Validation
└── Fold 3 → Testing
Split	Images	Nuclei
Fold 1 — Train	2,656	63,218
Fold 2 — Validation	2,523	59,872
Fold 3 — Test	2,722	66,654
Total	7,901	189,744

Each image is:

256 × 256 × 3

and represents an H&E-stained histopathology image patch.

2. Data files

Each fold contains:

images.npy
masks.npy
types.npy
images.npy
Shape: (N, 256, 256, 3)
dtype: float64

Contains the RGB H&E histopathology images.

The observed pixel range is approximately:

28 → 255
types.npy
Shape: (N,)
dtype: <U13

This contains one tissue type per image, not one label per nucleus.

There are 19 tissue types, including:

Breast
Colon
Liver
Lung
Kidney
Cervix
Prostate
...

The distribution is imbalanced, with Breast and Colon being the most common.

For example, across all folds:

Breast → 2,351 images
Colon  → 1,440 images

We will treat types.npy primarily as metadata, not as the target for our nucleus classification task.

3. masks.npy

This is the most important annotation file.

Shape:
(N, 256, 256, 6)

There are six channels:

Channel 0 → Neoplastic
Channel 1 → Inflammatory
Channel 2 → Connective
Channel 3 → Dead
Channel 4 → Epithelial
Channel 5 → Background

Channels 0–4 contain instance IDs, not probabilities.

For example:

0 0 7 7 0
0 7 7 7 0
0 0 7 0 0

means all pixels with value 7 belong to one nucleus instance.

Therefore:

100 pixels with ID 7
        ↓
1 nucleus

while:

100 pixels with ID 7
+
150 pixels with ID 12
        ↓
2 nuclei

This is how we calculated the nucleus-level class distribution.

4. Pixel-level imbalance

The masks are highly sparse.

For example, in Fold 1:

Class	Non-zero pixel %
Neoplastic	9.03%
Inflammatory	1.79%
Connective	3.70%
Dead	0.065%
Epithelial	2.45%
Background channel	81.61%

Therefore, most pixels do not belong to a particular nucleus class.

This is expected in histopathology segmentation because nuclei occupy relatively small regions of the image.

5. Nucleus-level class distribution

This is more important for our classification branch.

Across all three folds:

Nucleus class	Total nuclei	Percentage
Neoplastic	77,403	40.8%
Connective	50,585	26.7%
Inflammatory	32,276	17.0%
Epithelial	26,572	14.0%
Dead	2,908	1.5%
Total	189,744	100%

The major issue is class imbalance.

There are approximately:

77,403 Neoplastic
        vs
 2,908 Dead

So Neoplastic nuclei are roughly 26.6× more numerous than Dead nuclei.

This means we should consider class-weighted loss for the per-nucleus classification branch.

6. Important distinction we established

There are two different statistics:

Pixel-level statistic
9.03% Neoplastic pixels

means:

9.03% of all pixels belong to Neoplastic nucleus instances.

Instance-level statistic
26,201 Neoplastic nuclei in Fold 1

means:

There are 26,201 individual Neoplastic nucleus instances.

These are not interchangeable.

This distinction is critical because our eventual task is instance segmentation followed by per-nucleus classification.
